                  },
                  {
                    "ReferenceNumber": 21,
                    "Description": "PEER REVIEWED",
                    "Reference": [
                      "USEPA, Office of Prevention, Pesticides, and Toxic Substances; Revised HED Human Health Risk Assessment for Siduron (1982-46-2) (July 2008). EPA Docket No.: EPA-HQ-OPP-2007-0973-0019."
                    ],
                    "ExtendedReference": [
                      {
                        "Citation": "USEPA, Office of Prevention, Pesticides, and Toxic Substances; Revised HED Human Health Risk Assessment for Siduron (1982-46-2) (July 2008). EPA Docket No.: EPA-HQ-OPP-2007-0973-0019.",
                        "Matched": {
                          "PCLID": 900134642,
                          "Citation": "USEPA, Office of Prevention, Pesticides, and Toxic Substances; Revised HED Human Health Risk Assessment for Siduron (1982-46-2) (July 2008). EPA Docket No.: EPA-HQ-OPP-2007-0973-0019."
                        }
                      }
                    ],
                    "Value": {
                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "/LABORATORY ANIMALS: Subchronic or Prechronic Exposure/ ... Repeated- dose dermal toxicity studies with IN Z1318-70 in male rabbits /were/ conducted in two phases: Study I and Study II. Five animals in each group (4 control in Study I) were sampled (blood collected) following a 21- day recovery period. At the end of the exposure period 5 rabbits/ group were killed for general gross pathological and male reproductive organ histological examination. The remaining animals were killed following the 21- day recovery period and similarly evaluated ... In Study I, one animal in the 5 mg/kg group exhibited mild conjunctival redness of the right eye, and one animal in the 15 mg/kg group exhibited corneal opacity of the left eye. In both studies (I and II) the incidence and severity of dermal erythema (slight to severe) and edema (slight to moderate) increased with exposure concentration of IN Z1318-70. Only slight erythema was observed in the control groups. Superficial necrosis and/ or necrosis were observed predominantly in animals treated with 1500 mg/kg and were located either on or near the actual application site. The dermal effects (erythema, edema, necrosis, epidermal scaling) for the most part can be attributed to the mechanical effect resulting from a combination of a moist microenvironment, the granularity of the test material, the daily wrapping and unwrapping, and daily attempts to remove excess test material. Although the test material may have produced a concentration- dependent dermal irritation and/ or altered the integrity of the skin, the dermal effects were not toxicologically significant The LOAEL is 1500 mg/kg/day, based on the increased severity and duration occurrence of dermal effects (erythema, edema, necrosis, epidermal scaling) (it is noted that the dose is greater than the limit dose). The NOAEL is 50 mg/kg/day."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  },
