                "TOCHeading": "Environmental Fate/Exposure Summary",
                "Description": "This section summarizes the environmental fate/exposure of this chemical, including the natural and artificial sources of the chemical as a pollutant, its environmental fate, biodegradation, abiotic degradation, bioconcentration, etc.",
                "Information": [
                  {
                    "ReferenceNumber": 21,
                    "Description": "PEER REVIEWED",
                    "Value": {
                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "Siduron's production may result in its release to the environment through various waste streams; its use as a herbicide will result in its direct release to the environment. If released to air, a vapor pressure of 4.0X10-9 mm Hg at 25 °C indicates siduron will exist solely in the particulate phase in the atmosphere. Particulate-phase siduron will be removed from the atmosphere by wet or dry deposition. Siduron does not contain chromophores that absorb at wavelengths >290 nm and therefore is not expected to be susceptible to direct photolysis by sunlight. If released to soil, siduron is expected to have moderate mobility based upon estimated Koc values of 330-420. Volatilization from moist and dry soil surfaces is not expected to be an important fate process based upon an estimated Henry's Law constant of 6.8X10-11 atm-cu m/mole and its vapor pressure, respectively. Biodegradation is expected to be an important removal process in soil with half-lives ranging from 90-150 days. If released into water, siduron may be expected to adsorb to suspended solids and sediment based upon the estimated Koc. Microbial degradation in aquatic systems may be important based upon biodegradation studies in soil. Volatilization from water surfaces is not expected to be an important fate process based upon this compound's estimated Henry's Law constant. An estimated BCF of 150 suggests the potential for bioconcentration in aquatic organisms is high, provided the compound is not metabolized by the organism. Hydrolysis may occur under acidic or alkaline conditions. Occupational exposure to siduron may occur through inhalation and dermal contact with this compound at workplaces where siduron is produced or used. Use data indicate that the general population may be exposed to siduron via inhalation and dermal contact with consumer containing siduron. (SRC)",
                          "Markup": [
                            {
                              "Start": 0,
                              "Length": 7,
                              "URL": "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/Siduron",
                              "Type": "PubChem Internal Link",
